  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nIt's been a picture-perfectly-quiet Thanksgiving Day across\nsouth central Nebraska and north central Kansas and rather\npleasant with temperatures near 50F amidst light winds. Quiet\nweather will persist through Black Friday, as well, and\ntemperatures will even warm a few degrees over today. Winds will\nbe slightly breezier, esp. by afternoon, out of the SE at around\n10-15 MPH.\n\nThe main window for potentially impactful weather will come with\nour next upper disturbance Saturday into Saturday night. Models\nremain in fairly good agreement with the overall timing and\ntrack of the system. A band of scattered rain showers could\ndevelop along the warm air advection wing of the system anywhere\nover south central Nebraska as early as Saturday morning.\nHowever, the brunt of the upper lift/moisture will swing through\nthe region late Saturday into Saturday night. Most pieces of\nguidance continues to suggest a mostly all-rain event, though\nthe EC data (both deterministic and ensemble) still tries to\nchange the rain to a mix or even all (wet) snow after sunset\nSaturday evening. In fact, 12Z EPS places 40-60% chances for at\nleast an inch of wet snow accumulations for the NW third, or so,\nof the forecast area. Some of this snow would likely melt at the\nonset given both air and wet bulb temperatures slightly above\nfreezing...and most other guidance is even less than the 1-1.5\"\nfrom the EC. As such, official forecast remains on the lower end\nof the snow potential and mainly in the rain or rain/snow mix\ncamp with little to no accumulations. Will continue to monitor\nlatest trends and if models come around to the EC idea, it may\nbe worth inclusion into the HWO. Ensemble 25-75 percentile for\nQPF is generally between 0.10\" and 0.50\", highest N of I-80 and\nW of Hwy 281. Our KS counties may see little to no accumulation.\n\nOtherwise...the remainder of the forecast is very quiet and\nseasonable. Cool temperatures will linger behind the departing\nsystem on Sunday, but expect a gradual warmup back into the 50s\nfor Tuesday and Wednesday. The next system doesn't look to \narrive until late Wednesday or Thursday.",
